//! Numerical optimization on core's `Array`: roots and minima of scalar
//! functions, unconstrained minimization (simplex, Powell, conjugate
//! gradients, BFGS, L-BFGS, Newton-CG, trust regions), bounds (L-BFGS-B,
//! projected gradient), nonlinear constraints (augmented Lagrangian),
//! linear and convex quadratic programs (simplex, interior point),
//! nonlinear least squares and curve fitting (Levenberg-Marquardt,
//! trust-region reflective, robust losses), systems of equations
//! (Newton, Broyden, hybrid Powell) and global search (differential
//! evolution, basin hopping, simulated annealing).
//!
//! Derivatives come from the user, from automatic differentiation
//! (autodiff's `Dual` forward mode or `Var` reverse mode), from the
//! complex step, or from finite differences. Bad input throws an
//! `OptimizeError` that says what to write instead; a run that does not
//! converge returns its result with the reason in `status`.
//!
//! Pure Nabi on f64: every target, wasm included, gets the same results.
//!
//! # Fitting and optimization
//!
//! Fitting a model to data, finding the input that makes a cost smallest
//! and finding where a function is zero are one family of problems, and
//! `optimize` solves all three. Objectives are ordinary closures. Written
//! over `optimize::Dual` instead of `f64`, a closure gets its exact
//! gradient by automatic differentiation, so you never write a derivative
//! by hand.
//!
//! ## Find a root
//!
//! Where is `cos x = x`? `brentq` needs an interval where the function
//! changes sign, and from there it always converges. `newton` is faster
//! from a good start but needs the derivative; `newton_ad` takes a closure
//! over `Dual` and gets the derivative by itself.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use optimize::Dual;
//!
//! let f = |x: f64| -> f64 { x.cos() - x };
//! let r = try optimize::brentq(f, 0.0, 1.0);
//! println(f"cos x = x at x = {r.x:.12} ({r.iterations} iterations)");
//!
//! let g = |x: Dual| -> Dual { x.cos() - x };
//! let n = try optimize::newton_ad(g, 0.5);
//! println(f"newton: {n.x:.12} ({n.iterations} iterations)");
//! # assert((r.x - 0.739085133215).abs() < 1e-12 && r.iterations == 6);
//! # assert((n.x - 0.739085133215).abs() < 1e-12 && n.iterations == 4);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! cos x = x at x = 0.739085133215 (6 iterations)
//! newton: 0.739085133215 (4 iterations)
//! ```
//!
//! ## Minimize a function
//!
//! The Rosenbrock function has its minimum at (1, 1) at the bottom of a
//! long curved valley, which makes it a standard test. `bfgs` follows the
//! gradient and builds a picture of the curvature as it goes. The result
//! says how it stopped, where and after how many steps.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use optimize::Dual;
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let rosen = |x: Vector<Dual>| -> Dual { 100.0 * (x[1] - x[0] ** 2) ** 2 + (1.0 - x[0]) ** 2 };
//! let m = try optimize::bfgs(rosen, [-1.2, 1.0]);
//! println(f"{m.status:?} at x = {m.x:.6}, f = {m.fun:.1e}, {m.iterations} iterations");
//! # assert(f"{m.status:?}" == "Converged" && m.iterations == 37);
//! # assert((m.x[0] - 1.0).abs() < 5e-7 && (m.x[1] - 1.0).abs() < 5e-7 && m.fun < 1e-17);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! Converged at x = [1.000000, 1.000000], f = 3.8e-19, 37 iterations
//! ```
//!
//! With bounds on the variables, use `lbfgsb`; with general constraints,
//! `minimize` and its methods; without any gradient at all (a simulation, a
//! measurement), `nelder_mead`. A plain closure over `f64` works everywhere
//! too, with the gradient by finite differences.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use optimize::{Bounds, Dual};
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let rosen = |x: Vector<Dual>| -> Dual { 100.0 * (x[1] - x[0] ** 2) ** 2 + (1.0 - x[0]) ** 2 };
//! let box = try Bounds::new([-2.0, -2.0], [0.5, 2.0]);
//! let m = try optimize::lbfgsb(rosen, [-1.2, 1.0], box);
//! println(f"inside the box: x = {m.x:.4}");
//!
//! let nm = try optimize::nelder_mead(|x: Vector<f64>| -> f64 { (x[0] - 3.0) ** 2 + (x[1] + 1.0) ** 2 }, [0.0, 0.0]);
//! println(f"no gradient: x = {nm.x:.4}");
//! # assert((m.x[0] - 0.5).abs() < 5e-5 && (m.x[1] - 0.25).abs() < 5e-5);
//! # assert((nm.x[0] - 3.0).abs() < 5e-5 && (nm.x[1] + 1.0).abs() < 5e-5);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! inside the box: x = [0.5000, 0.2500]
//! no gradient: x = [3.0000, -1.0000]
//! ```
//!
//! ## Fit a curve
//!
//! `curve_fit` adjusts the parameters of a model `y = f(x; p)` until it
//! matches the data in the least-squares sense, and reports a standard
//! error for each parameter. The model takes one `x` and the parameter
//! vector. Here the decay of a radioactive sample: counts per minute over
//! time, with a constant background.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use optimize::Dual;
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let t: Vector<f64> = [0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60];
//! let counts: Vector<f64> = [1011, 760, 590, 455, 362, 238, 169, 128, 110];
//!
//! // counts = a e^(-t / tau) + c
//! let model = |t: f64, p: Vector<Dual>| -> Dual { p[0] * (-t / p[1]).exp() + p[2] };
//! let fit = try optimize::curve_fit(model, t, counts, [1000.0, 20.0, 50.0]);
//! let (a, tau, c) = (fit.params[0], fit.params[1], fit.params[2]);
//! println(f"a = {a:.0}, tau = {tau:.2} min, background {c:.0}");
//! println(f"half-life {tau * 2.0f64.ln():.2} min +- {fit.stderr[1] * 2.0f64.ln():.2}");
//! # assert((a - 921.0).abs() < 0.5 && (tau - 16.45).abs() < 0.005 && (c - 87.0).abs() < 0.5);
//! # assert((tau * 2.0f64.ln() - 11.40).abs() < 0.005 && (fit.stderr[1] * 2.0f64.ln() - 0.13).abs() < 0.005);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! a = 921, tau = 16.45 min, background 87
//! half-life 11.40 min +- 0.13
//! ```
//!
//! Give the starting guess some thought: a nonlinear fit finds the minimum
//! nearest to where it starts. Plotting the data first is usually enough.
//! For a model that is linear in its parameters (a polynomial, a sum of
//! known functions), [least
//! squares](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/linalg/latest) or
//! [`stats::polyfit`](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/stats/latest)
//! need no guess at all.
//!
//! ## Linear programming
//!
//! A linear program maximizes or minimizes a linear cost under linear
//! constraints: a factory makes two products, each needing machine time and
//! labour, and has only so much of each. `Lp` builds the problem row by row
//! (`le` for `<=` constraints); `linprog` minimizes, so a profit to
//! maximize goes in with its sign flipped.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use optimize::Lp;
//!
//! // Profit 30 per chair and 50 per table. A chair takes 1 h of machine time
//! // and 2 h of labour, a table 2 h and 3 h; 40 machine hours and 70 labour
//! // hours are available.
//! let lp = Lp::new([-30.0, -50.0]).le([[1, 2], [2, 3]], [40.0, 70.0]);
//! let r = try optimize::linprog(lp);
//! println(f"chairs {r.x[0]:.0}, tables {r.x[1]:.0}, profit {-r.fun:.0}");
//! # assert((r.x[0] - 20.0).abs() < 1e-6 && (r.x[1] - 10.0).abs() < 1e-6 && (r.fun + 1100.0).abs() < 1e-6);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! chairs 20, tables 10, profit 1100
//! ```
//!
//! `quadprog` solves problems with a quadratic cost. Integer variables are
//! not supported.
//!
//! ## What next
//!
//! - [Differential
//!   equations](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/ode/latest): models
//!   that are equations for a rate of change.
//! - [Derivatives](https://packages.nabi-lang.org/docs/autodiff/latest):
//!   the automatic differentiation behind `Dual`, on your own functions.
//! - The items below: every method of `minimize` (`Method`), the `Options`,
//!   and the global optimizers `differential_evolution`, `basin_hopping`
//!   and `simulated_annealing`.

